<asp:Content ContentPlaceHolderID="MainContent" runat="server">

    <img src="20240804_000000.jpg" alt="Flight path from Sky Harbor Airport to Kahului Airport" />

	<p id='Extract'>Even though I spent three weeks in Kauai with my daughter Jenny and 
		the grandkids just last month, when Rob&mdash;the guy who bought Jenny's property&mdash;texted 
		me that he had found a box of my photos I would probably want, I couldn't resist. 
		Of course, it would have been far cheaper for me to send him money to take the box 
		to the airport than for me to come and get it. But with my upcoming hip replacement 
		surgery, I thought, <i>Who knows when I'll even be able to visit Hawaii again, 
		if ever?</i> So I hopped on a plane&mdash;correction, was <i>wheeled</i> onto a plane&mdash;and 
		the next thing I knew, I was flying once more to Kahului Airport and my favorite island.</p>

	<img src="20240804_000001.jpg" />

	<p>The reason for the hip replacement is that I can hardly walk, which is why I had to
		request wheelchair service. As I may have mentioned in a 
		<a href="../../2024-06/24.Just_Say_No_To_Oak_Street_Health/">previous post</a>, this was my 
		second attempt at scheduling the hip replacement. I needed it badly in June, and my
		mobility has only decreased since then. So I didn't plan this to be a hiking adventure.
		Nor did I plan to stay long, as of course I am on a Social Security budget.
		I did not make any arrangements for accommodations, because I intended to sleep in
		my rental car.</p>
    
	<img src='20240804_000002.jpg' />

	<img src='20240804_000003.png' class="Right"/>

	<p>They also wheeled me from my arrival gate to baggage claim. The wheelchair guy offered to
		wait with me, but I didn't think it would be necessary. I was wrong, and the baggage department
		had no place to sit while waiting for my bag to come out.</p>

	<p>I had made arrangements to rent my vehicle from <a href="https://cruisinmaui.com/">Cruisin' Maui Car Rentals</a>,
		which is located off the actual airport, which may be why their prices were so reasonable. They pick
		you up at the airport, which is the good news; the bad news is that the airport requires rental car and Uber pickups
		to be done a bit of a stroll to get to. (Nothing that would ever have bothered me when my hip and I
		got along better.)</p>

	<p>While I'm talking about them, I should mention they were very nice and very helpful, and even loaned me
		a mattress for the SUV I rented, as well as a blanket and pillow!</p>

	<img src='20240804_000005.jpg' />

	<p>So here's a map of Maui, showing my route from Kahului to <a href="www.campolowalu.com">Camp Olowalu</a>.
		This is a place I camped at more than once, when I lived here and the pandemic was winding down.
		Back then, though, I mostly overnighted at Papalaua Beach Park. But I've heard they've kind of cracked
		down on that since then. Anyway, I also wanted a nice bathroom (Papalaua has Porta-Potties) and shower,
		and Camp Olowalu offers those.</p>

	<img src='20240804_000011.png' />

	<p>It's not glitzy and it's certainly no luxury resort, like the Hyatt, Hilton, and Westin chains do
		(and just a few miles away)! But a night at the Hyatt would run $532, while Oluwalu is just $50.</p>

	<p>By the way, "Oluwalu" is correctly pronounced with a V sound replacing the W.
		The first attempts to transcribe the Hawaiian language using the Latin alphabet were made by 
		British explorer James Cook and his crew during their visit to the Hawaiian Islands in 1778. 
		They recorded the island name as "Owhyhee" or "Owhyee," which phonetically resembles "Hawaii". 
		This early transcription effort laid the groundwork for future linguists and missionaries who 
		arrived in the early 19th century. The missionaries, particularly those from the American 
		Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions (ABCFM), worked with native Hawaiian scholars 
		to develop a standardized written form of the language. By 1826, they had established a 
		12-letter alphabet consisting of five vowels (a, e, i, o, u) and seven consonants 
		(h, k, l, m, n, p, w), using the <i>Latin</i> pronunciation of those letters. 
		This system allowed for the accurate representation of Hawaiian sounds, 
		resulting in spellings like "Hawaii" (Hav eye ee) and "Oluwalu" (Oh loo vahl oo)&mdash;if
		you happen to be a 17th century, Latin-oriented missionary. None of us are, so
		today mostly tourists pronounce the Ws, while the locals know better.</p>

	<img src='20240804_000012.jpg' />

	<p>Back in 2021, car camping at Olowalu meant just parking in a lot and going to sleep.
		But it's much nicer now! They have a special area for car camping, with privacy bushes
		between parking spaces, a nice picnic table, and of course the aforementioned nearby
		flush toilets and showers.</p>

    <img src="20240806_072939.jpg" />
	<img src='20240804_184003.jpg' />

	<p>Taking a quick tour of the grounds while heading for the showers, I spotted the new glamping
		cabins. In retrospect, that might have been more comfortable than the SUV. But, of course,
		also more expensive.</p>

    <img src="20240806_155213.jpg" />

	<p>From pretty much anywhere on the grounds, if you look North you can see the hills 
		of the West Maui mountains.</p>
	
	<img src="20240806_153804.jpg" />

	<p>And in the opther direction, there's the shore! They don't really have a proper beach; it's
		pretty rocky and narrow. But it's awesome for sitting on a tree branch or picnic table
		and just enjoying the ocean, sounds, smells and sights.</p>

    <img src="20240806_154059.jpg" />
    <img src="20240806_155046.jpg" />
	
	<p>One of my favorite things about Maui is all the flowering trees. And they've planted (or
		worked around) some beauties!</p>

	<img src="20240806_160409.jpg" />
    <img src="20240806_160711.jpg" />

	<p>Then of course there are the feral chickens that inhabit the island.
		The feral chickens on Maui have an interesting history. Their origins can be traced back 
		to the original Polynesian settlers who brought the Red Junglefowl, a tropical bird in the
		pheasant family, to the islands centuries ago. Red Junglefowl, after much breeding,
		became the ancestor of today's domesticated chicken. Over time, these chickens escaped 
		and interbred further with native birds. Additionally, hurricanes like Iwa in 1982 and 
		Iniki in 1992 caused damage to chicken coops, allowing more domesticated chickens to escape into the wild.</p>

    <img src="20240806_153506.jpg" />

	<p>Maui lacks significant predators that could effectively control the chicken population. 
		True, locals often kill and eat feral chickens but not even Colonel Sanders could keep up.
		This absence of natural predation, along with abundant food sources and lenient regulations, 
		has allowed the feral chicken population to thrive.</p>

	<img src='20240804_184132.jpg' />

	<p>Besides, they eat bugs and are endearing to watch.</p>

	<img src='20240804_184531.jpg' />

	<p>Remembering that Hawaii is 3 hours behind Phoenix, and I'd just been on a six-and-a-half hour flight,
		and then driven to Oluwalu and checked out&mdash;well, as you might imagine I was exhausted
		and definitely ready to turn in for the night. I managed to keep awake through most of the
		sunset, reflected off the West Maui Mountains.</p>

	<img src='20240804_184557.jpg' />
	<img src='20240804_184824.jpg' />

	<p>Tomorrow I'll give Rob a call and see when he wants to get together for the picture box transfer.
		First, though, it's definitely time for sleep!</p>

